{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-48352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5]?$\n\n- $f(x)={x}^{3}$\n- $g(x)=|x|$\n- $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-48352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{3}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0$ but this point does not lie inside $(1,5).$\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ is not defined at $x=1$, so it is not continuous at $x=1$, which means it is not continuous on $[1,5].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-48352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "374bb6edf5e57c21aa77f6699efe016d87b5b97e3e537b225b290f16d2251808"} {"question" "q-48352" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-48352" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "All"] ["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-48352" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-77865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-4,4]?$\n\n- $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}$\n- $g(x)={2}^{1/{x}^{2}}$\n- $h(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-77865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-4,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[-4,4]$ and differentiable on $(-4,4).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-4,4]$ and differentiable on $(-4,4).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)={2}^{1/{x}^{2}}$ is not defined at $x=0,$ hence, it is not continuous at \n$x=0,$ and so it is not continuous on $[-4,4].$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}$ is continuous on $[-4,\\infty )$ and differentiable on $(-4,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-4,4]$ and differentiable on $(-4,4).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions I and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-4,4].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-77865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "374bb6edf5e57c21aa77f6699efe016d87b5b97e3e537b225b290f16d2251808"} {"question" "q-77865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-77865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-77865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-127011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-127011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $m(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $n(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $n(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $l(x)$  has jump discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $l(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)$  has removable discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-127011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "374bb6edf5e57c21aa77f6699efe016d87b5b97e3e537b225b290f16d2251808"} {"question" "q-127011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-127011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/4c9f5d5245a1a4aa33a0d474ff328a30a1c8820055551e3613bf7c9489c2a908.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/787772f6d68af4df373cbfdd6b7a3cf3d313db7e03ecce36437f01068b738408.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/8d37dc99a006ba89bc6a874d251a065fc714b260072740206f9cc3692b08c604.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/d5927b5cc43ee1b5923b4d2aa4b4740e2bbc1c73b09b00525cb74767badc4808.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/f5d7744d62f94b96b8bd1600ca7f5fc752d1f83c8c8928e740261e808cb9e07b.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-127011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/4c9f5d5245a1a4aa33a0d474ff328a30a1c8820055551e3613bf7c9489c2a908.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-345310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find  the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x)=2{log}_{2}⁡x$ on the interval $[1,4].$\n\n$c=$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-345310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[1,4]$ and differentiable on $(1,4),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (1,4)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=1$ and $b=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡4-2{log}_{2}⁡1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-0}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{log}_{2}⁡x)=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{2}{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ c\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ c & =\\frac{3}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-345310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "374bb6edf5e57c21aa77f6699efe016d87b5b97e3e537b225b290f16d2251808"} {"question" "q-345310" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-345310" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2\\ln(2)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-345312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x)={x}^{4}-x$ on the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$c=$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-345312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem.  \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (-1,1)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=-1$ and $b=1$ into the above gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(1)-f(-1)}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{({1}^{4}-1)-((-1)^{4}-(-1))}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}-x)=4{x}^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=4{c}^{3}-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{c}^{3}-1 & =-1 \\\\ c & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-345312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "374bb6edf5e57c21aa77f6699efe016d87b5b97e3e537b225b290f16d2251808"} {"question" "q-345312" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"} {"question" "q-345312" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "51b737687ad85b179ec5795b415af02100449e8384083476936f10d8fa9634d4"}] "usage" {"q-48352" []} "basis" 1865 "retractions" [[17592186064730 :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1, 5]$?\n\n1. $f(x) = x^{3}$\n2. $g(x) = \\mid x \\mid$\n3. $h(x) = \\ln (x - 1)$"]] "immutable